{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve \\(3^{-1} + 13^{-1} \\pmod{19}\\), we need to find the modular inverses of 3 and 13 modulo 19, and then add them together, reducing the result modulo 19.\n\n### Step 1: Find \\(3^{-1} \\pmod{19}\\)\nThe modular inverse of 3 modulo 19 is a number \\(x\\) such that:\n\\[\n3x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{19}\n\\]\n\nWe can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. We start by applying the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 3 and 19.\n\n\\[\n19 = 6 \\cdot 3 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n3 = 3 \\cdot 1 + 0\n\\]\n\nThe gcd is 1, and we can backtrack to express 1 as a combination of 3 and 19:\n\\[\n1 = 19 - 6 \\cdot 3\n\\]\n\nThis shows that:\n\\[\n-6 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{19}\n\\]\n\nThus, the modular inverse of 3 modulo 19 is:\n\\[\n3^{-1} \\equiv -6 \\pmod{19}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(-6\\) is not in the range from 0 to 18, we convert it to a positive equivalent:\n\\[\n-6 + 19 = 13\n\\]\n\nSo:\n\\[\n3^{-1} \\equiv 13 \\pmod{19}\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":891,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,17767,18,87210,16,92,488,220,16,18,87210,16,92,1124,79,2593,90,16,24,11035,701,582,1184,311,1477,279,43893,64583,288,315,220,18,323,220,16,18,59864,220,16,24,11,323,1221,912,1105,3786,11,17719,279,1102,59864,220,16,24,382,14374,14822,220,16,25,7379,17767,18,87210,16,92,1124,79,2593,90,16,24,11035,340,785,43893,27949,315,220,18,59864,220,16,24,374,264,1372,17767,87,57758,1741,429,510,59,9640,18,87,1124,57317,220,16,1124,79,2593,90,16,24,532,59,2533,1654,646,990,279,40565,21963,50078,40325,311,1477,419,27949,13,1205,1191,553,18950,279,21963,50078,40325,311,1477,279,12196,4185,49109,320,91289,8,315,220,18,323,220,16,24,382,59,9640,16,24,284,220,21,1124,50853,220,18,488,220,16,198,59,921,59,9640,18,284,220,18,1124,50853,220,16,488,220,15,198,59,2533,785,44858,374,220,16,11,323,582,646,98921,311,3158,